  43d345a8121205eb8a425ee1a96f1e012b85b450 2026-10-16 Fix #335. This new fix to the setjmp-in-a-loop bug brings the hammer down about as hard as possible: for every setjmp location, we remember if we have allocated a zjmp_buf within the frame, and if we had, then subsequence setjmps just overwrite it. This means that multiple executions of the same setjmp in a frame alias the same zjmp_buf. So, jumping back in time always really jumps to the latest setjmp. This ensures agreement between callee saves and spills, fully neutralizing the issue. This also reverts much of 96a4384a2dea4e76a9103666fda3bd776014c291, so we no longer need a weak set to track jmp_bufs. Hence, rather than risking any performance regression to fix the bug, we are making setjmp faster by doing this. As weird as it is, this fix is correct, since it gives equivalent behavior to the compiler simply not allocating callee saves across the setjmp (something that the compiler always had the right to do if it wanted). But, we achieve as-if that behavior without actually blocking the compiler from doing whatever register allocation it wants to do - so we're not hurting perf, and we're not making the compiler any more complex. This also reverts the non-test portion of 995fb4c7e4fdc400b7c419591829774576bf6f0d, since we no longer need to MakeSetjmpsGreatAgain or do any of the other nonsense. NOTE: in its current form, this fix is an ABI break!
  995fb4c7e4fdc400b7c419591829774576bf6f0d 2026-10-13 Fix a bunch of issues having to do with setjmps in loops. If a setjmp is in a loop, and we longjmp to a prior loop iteration, then any values live in spills will keep the value from the jumped-from loop iteration. Prior to this fix, the lowers (used for GC tracking) would revert to the values in the jumped-to loop iteration (i.e. the older loop iteration). Worse, a flight pointer might get torn: its intval might be in a callee save while its capability is spilled, or vice versa, meaning that the pointer traps on any access (this is memory-safe, but confusing to users). This change makes it so that loop-defined values live across a setjmp in a loop are always spilled and the lowers are always placed in unique lowers slots that get restored after the setjmp. This ensures that values live across setjmps in loops always get the jumped-from value in all of the places that matter: flight pointer intval, lower, and the GC tracking lower copy. This also ensures that if there are any emergent SSA values post pizlonation that the compiler assumes are equal to themselves, then they will still be equal to themselves after a longjmp back to a setjmp-in-a-loop. Bug found by Anthropic. One of the test cases courtesy of Anthropic. I did not take the Anthropic-provided fix; that fix didn't go far enough (didn't handle the emergent SSA values issue) and also went too far (GC allocated all variables, and I think it didn't restrict itself to the in-a-loop variables/setjmps). I added a lot of tests for various diabolical variants of this bug.
  e33a8e2c5376f476ad9ea72c0ca02265504f18bb 2026-10-03 Fix #330. This makes clang's lowering of null constants for unions that have member pointers and normal pointers conform to the new way of representing unions with pointers.
